Patch to GDE GoZen (applied last by build/gozen/build_gozen.sh): VAAPI hardware decoding on Linux x86_64 (Intel Quick
Sync through intel-media-va-driver, AMD through Mesa). The same code path as Windows' Direct3D 11 Video / DXVA2
(gozen.patch `_open_hwaccel()`): FFmpeg's own h264/hevc/vp9/vp8/mpeg4 decoder with a VAAPI device, the frames
copied to memory as NV12 (`_hw_download()`), software when the GPU cannot decode the video. GOZEN_HWDEC=0 turns it
off, GOZEN_HWDEC=vaapi asks for it only, GOZEN_VAAPI_DEVICE=/dev/dri/renderD129 picks another render node. The arm64
builds have no VAAPI hardware config, so nothing changes there. SConstruct links libva and libva-drm on x86_64.

diff --git a/SConstruct b/SConstruct
index e5c9e50..c5a0209 100644
--- a/SConstruct
+++ b/SConstruct
@@ -39,6 +39,10 @@ if "linux" in platform:
     # (and with FFmpeg's assembly, whose static objects need -Bsymbolic to link into a shared library)
     if ARGUMENTS.get("lean", "no") == "yes":
         env.Append(LINKFLAGS=["-Wl,-Bsymbolic"])
+        # x86_64: FFmpeg built with --enable-vaapi (Intel Quick Sync, AMD); libva2 and libva-drm2 are loaded when the
+        # extension is (the Linux install line installs them)
+        if arch == "x86_64":
+            env.Append(LIBS=["va", "va-drm"])
     else:
         env.Append(LIBS=["tls", "ssl", "crypto", "vpx", "aom"])
 
